          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"skram-lucie-388b107bcaa8cfbe\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.94,\"analysis\":\"Lucie directly and emphatically avows her love for Theodor. The exclamative wording, whisper, kiss, and physical intimacy intensify this truth-conditional avowal but do not independently show that the words are a reflex-like expressive discharge. She undertakes no commitment or obligation here.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The exclamative “Aa gud som” and immediate erotic tenderness permit a weak expressive reading, but the text supplies no blurt-like, involuntary, or otherwise independently reflexive framing.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Lucie directly whispers “Aa gud som jeg elsker Dig, Theodor” to Theodor during their reconciliation.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"The declaration occurs during an intimate reconciliation after Theodor returns to Lucie and kisses her. Lucie combines the avowal with a complaint that he is cruel to her, making it affectionate but also vulnerable and reproachful. Her subsequent willingness to consider marrying Nilsen for security despite loving Theodor further distinguishes this emotional avowal from any marital or future-oriented undertaking.\" ,\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Aa gud som jeg elsker Dig, Theodor”, hvisked hun.\",\"supports\":\"The explicit first-person present-tense declaration strongly supports truth-conditional avowal; the whisper and exclamative form indicate intensity but not by themselves independent expressive/reflexive force.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Immediately before speaking, Lucie puts an arm around Theodor and seeks his mouth with her lips during their reconciliation.\",\"supports\":\"The intimate action supports an affectionate and plausibly sincere contextual reading of the avowal.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Du ved det ikke ... endda Du er så lej og slem imod mig” (“you do not know it, even though you are so nasty and cruel to me”).\",\"supports\":\"Lucie presents her love as an existing emotional state that persists despite his mistreatment, reinforcing T rather than an undertaking.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Later Lucie says that marrying Nilsen would be sensible and imagines having to work to forget Theodor.\",\"supports\":\"The wider scene distinguishes her love for Theodor from practical marital commitment, weighing against P in the classified utterance.\",\"confidence\":0.92}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the utterance as an emphatic avowal of an existing loving state. Its emotional intensity and relational context can be described contextually without adding E, P, or O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
